trace_id": "step_13", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_14", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_15", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_01", "role": "confounder_control"}], "narrative": "1006 and 1860 are both low-degree nodes (5 events each) in a sparse, almost non-repeating graph (7200 events, repeat ratio 0.016). 1006's out-activity is confined to a burst in [20631,21430] (three events, to 45/230/925/1680) plus isolated events near 40832-42031 and 46832-48031. 1860's inbound events occur at [19231,20430] (before 1006 acted), then not again until [26432,27631] (from 182), [32432,33631] (from 1390) and [43232,44431] (from 153). The nearest candidate target event therefore sits ~5000 time units after 1006's last burst event, which a 600-unit-gap chain could only bridge with ~9 consecutive perfectly-timed hops; the 600-unit slices of 1006's out-neighbourhood (step_14) and of 1860's in-neighbourhood (step_15) show only sparse, non-contiguous activity rather than a relayed chain. For 1006's later activity, the full 2-hop reachable set in [40832,44431] is just {563,1075,1664} \u2014 it contains neither 1860 nor any of its in-neighbours (153/182/958/1390), so the 43232-44431 contact from 153 cannot descend from 1006. Static 2-hop connectivity does exist through hubs 45/153/182 (step_03), but that is an order-ignoring artefact and is controlled for here. Conclusion: no genuine time-respecting cascade with max gap 600 from 1006 to 1860 in this window."}, "raw_final_response": "```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_636b848d\",\n  \"decision\": \"absent\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"cascade\",\n  \"entities\": [1006, 1860],\n  \"window\": [18031, 56431],\n  \"confidence\": 0.6,\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_02\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_04\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_05\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_06\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"s